  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 223 PM EDT Tue Sep 22 2026\n\nAfternoon RAP analysis has steep midlevel ridging over the north-\ncentral CONUS and into central Canada, with surface high pressure of \n1034mb centered over the Ontario/Quebec border. This is helping to \nsuppress a shortwave well to our south, over northern IL, with sunny \nskies over most of the UP. Visible satellite does show some fair \nweather cumulus development over Menominee county,but this should \ndiminish heading into the evening hours. Temperatures, meanwhile, \nare climbing into the upper 50s to lower 60s area-wide so far, and \nshould peak in the lower to mid 60s for most today. Dewpoints are \nmixing out quite nicely, already into the upper 30s and lower 40s \nacross portions of the interior UP. This would support some pockets \nwhere RH drops as low as 35-40% this afternoon, but light winds help \nto mitigate fire weather concerns. \n\nTemperatures plummet into the 30s across the interior of the UP \nagain tonight, coldest over the interior-west, with clear skies and \nlight winds. Model soundings show a very dry airmass, but will note \na brief period where lower-level moisture profiles show some \nsaturation, which could support frost development and even some \npatchy fog. Have opted to issue another Frost Advisory late tonight \ninto early Wednesday morning. \n\nDry, sunny, and quiet weather persists Thursday and Friday, \nalongside a slight increase in temperatures as the surface high \nshifts eastward and we come under mainly southerly flow. Expect \nhighs in the mid and upper 60s Thursday, followed by warmer \ntemperatures possibly near 70 Friday afternoon. Model soundings \nstill support a dewpoint drop during the afternoon hours, but will \nnote that more midlevel moisture is apparent compared to previous \ndays. So, minimum RH should still remain above 40% for both days. \nLooking ahead into the weekend, latest deterministic and ensemble \nguidance shows the midlevel ridge axis sliding eastward into Quebec, \nwith deeper troughing moving from the Pacific Northwest towards the \nprairie provinces. In between, a shortwave ejecting out of the \nPlains may scrape the western UP with some rain showers late \nSaturday into Sunday, while most of the UP likely remains dry for \nthe remainder of the forecast period.",
